(Chapter 4: Acid-Base Equilibrium and Buffer Systems) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The mechanism by which buffer solutions resist changes in pH depends on the) Tj T*
(equilibrium between a weak acid and its conjugate base. When a strong acid is) Tj T*
(added to the buffer, the conjugate base reacts with the excess protons, shifting) Tj T*
(the equilibrium and minimizing the resulting change in pH.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A common trap students fall into is assuming buffer capacity is unlimited.) Tj T*
(In reality, once the weak acid or conjugate base is fully consumed, the) Tj T*
(buffer system loses its ability to resist pH changes, and the solution) Tj T*
(pH will change rapidly with further addition of acid or base.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Clinically, this principle explains why the bicarbonate buffer system in) Tj T*
(blood plasma is critical: small disruptions in the ratio of bicarbonate to) Tj T*
(carbonic acid can cause significant changes in blood pH, leading to acidosis) Tj T*
(or alkalosis. Diagnosis of these conditions often requires interpreting) Tj T*
(arterial blood gas values relative to the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Why this matters: the DAT and MCAT both test buffer calculations heavily,) Tj T*
(and understanding the underlying mechanism \(not just memorizing the formula\)) Tj T*
(is the key to answering multi-step equilibrium questions correctly.) Tj T*
